Emscripten platform support in the C runtime template (bend2/comp.ts).
Every change is under #if defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__), so native builds
compile the same C and behave as before. The target is wasm32 with
-sALLOW_MEMORY_GROWTH=0 and one worker (docs/decisions/0001 of bend-wasm).

- Term stack overflow: pool_stack maps the stack with a PROT_NONE guard
  page and catches SIGSEGV/SIGBUS on a sigaltstack; Emscripten's mprotect
  is a stub and wasm raises no signals, so an overflow would overwrite
  memory silently. Under Emscripten pool_stack has no guard page and sets
  no signal handlers, and WL_ROOM (on native a check on the device only)
  stops before a frame would pass the stack's end with ERR_DEEP, the same
  "memory fault (machine stack overflow?)" as the native signal handler.
  Every push of the Term stack pointer sp on the host, and its cover:
  a segment's frame before a call (the emitter's STK(j) stores and
  WL_PUSHN) follows WL_ROOM(frame length) on every path; FID_ENTER's
  WL_FRAME and WL_ARGS, and FID_EXIT's (after its pop back to the base),
  push unchecked but always from the stack's base (every work_loop call
  passes the stack itself), at most 3 + 247 words (WIDE bounds a fid's
  arity) = 2,000 bytes, inside the 16 KiB reserved past stk_end; WL_CONT
  and WL_IDX write below sp; WL_RETN, WL_POPN and FID_EXIT's pop lower
  it. No push was left uncovered.
- Host layout: one worker on the host keeps its allocator row in the
  static ALC[w] and its Term stack in pool_stack, so the corpus leaves
  out the device's per-lane allocator rows (8 MiB) and Term stacks
  (256 MiB), and holds the rings (CUBE * RING_WORDS words, 128.25 MiB)
  only when BANGS != 0: a program without ! forks runs work_loop with seq
  set and never deals a task. RING_OFF is ALC_OFF and STAT_OFF follows
  the rings, if any. corpus_lay's cap > CUBE (a heap page per device
  lane) is a GPU requirement, so on the host it asks for two pages (page
  0 is heap_alloc_miss's scratch page; running out later is ERR_HEAP).
  CUBE_T, CUBE_LOG, RING_LEN and so the ! schedule stay as they are (a
  smaller CUBE_T would change the fork order, and RING_WORDS and
  RING_LOG's 17 assume 128). Offsets never reach output (locations are
  word indices from the corpus base; bend-wasm's docs/memory.md, part 2).
- Defaults (still overridable with -D): BEND_STACK_BYTES 8 MiB and
  BEND_CORPUS_BYTES sized from need, 8 * (HEAP_OFF + 1,280 * (PAGE_LEN +
  10)) bytes rounded up to 16 KiB: the layout below the heap (header,
  rings if any, static image) and 1,280 heap pages with their 10 bank
  words each. Robokill's simulation reaches 305 pages (39,040 words) in
  its replay and in every worst input built from its own step.bend (2,048
  bodies in dense collisions, 239 live projectiles, the most their
  240-frame life allows, every aim; 129 runs), and 481 pages with two
  states alive at once; bend-wasm's differential programs and probes
  reach at most 129 (its out-of-memory probe aside). 1,280 pages are 40
  of the 32-page quanta the allocator takes per size class, 4.2 times
  Robokill's need: a factor of 4 so that a game's state can grow by a
  few kinds of objects, or a host can hold a second state, before it
  meets ERR_HEAP, at a cost of untouched pages only. For a program without ! forks and a small static
  image that is 1,425,408 bytes (1.36 MiB); with ! forks the rings add
  134,479,872 bytes (about 129.6 MiB in all), of which a run touches only
  the first slot rows. A full corpus stops with the runtime's
  out-of-memory error (ERR_HEAP); a -DBEND_CORPUS_BYTES too small for the
  layout stops at start (bend_init, or main) with its own error.
  The stack default is 8 MiB, the stack of patch 0001's wasm32 run. The
  differential corpus does not bound it from below: every program that
  runs on wasm32 at 8 MiB (48 of 51) also runs at 512 bytes (2^9), and
  2^8 stops float_printing and fork_shared_flat with ERR_DEEP; no program
  of the corpus is deep. 8 MiB holds a million one-word frames
  (bend-wasm's probe/deep.bend pushes 20 million and overflows it).
- One worker: main forces the thread count to 1, and --threads above 1
  fails with "more than one thread is not supported on this target", so
  pool_open starts no pthread.
- The corpus and the Term stack are static zero-initialized arrays
  (corpus_bss of BEND_CORPUS_BYTES, pool_stack_bss of BEND_STACK_BYTES +
  16 KiB), not mmap: Emscripten's mmap is memalign plus a memset, which
  made every byte resident and cost bend_init its time. Static storage
  is enough because the runtime needs zeros only in the corpus header
  [0, ALC_OFF) and the rings' get/put words (a fresh corpus with any
  other bytes elsewhere gives identical results; bend-wasm's
  docs/memory.md, part 3), non-shared wasm memory is zero at
  instantiation and wasm-ld writes no data for .bss, and nothing reuses
  the arrays: corpus_setup and pool_stack run once per instance
  (bend_init refuses a second call, one worker), corpus_grow fails, and
  re-instantiating the module gives fresh memory. A shared-memory
  (pthread) build would zero .bss with memory.fill at start: still
  correct, only slower.
- corpus_grow fails at once, so a full corpus ends in the runtime's
  out-of-memory error (ERR_HEAP): memory growth is off and the corpus is
  taken once. Its text there names the corpus size and
  -DBEND_CORPUS_BYTES instead of the --gpu flag a wasm build lacks
  (err_post; ERRS in comp.ts, shared with the JS lane, is unchanged).
- Unsupported effects fail loudly when reached, through err_fail:
  io_wait_on (every timer or stream wait that would lead to io_wait's
  blocking select), io_work (the helper pthread of files, processes and
  random numbers) and io_sys_addr (where every socket effect starts).
  IO.random_u32 and Process.run (once its arguments pass its checks) run
  only on the helper pthread, so both reach io_work's error;
  so that they compile, effs/random_u32.c also includes sys/random.h
  under Emscripten (getrandom is declared there) and effs/process_run.c
  skips posix_spawn_file_actions_addclosefrom_np, which Emscripten's libc
  does not declare.
  Printing (io_out) is unchanged.
- Segments take Env as two pointers: WL_SIG and WL_ALL start with
  WL_ENV_SIG and WL_ENV_ALL, which on native are "Env e" and "e" as
  before; under Emscripten they are Env's two words, and WL_OPEN rebuilds
  e from them. Clang's wasm32 and wasm64 ABI pass a struct by a pointer
  to a copy in the caller's frame, and a musttail call (WL_JMP, WL_DYN)
  frees that frame before the callee reads it, so a segment read a
  clobbered Env: e.alc became 0 and heap_alloc took free chains from
  address 0 (reg/nat_chain and run/bst_insert ran out of memory,
  run/string_algorithms stopped with a stack overflow, UBSan "load of
  address 0x00000008" in heap_alloc).

Retired by: an upstream Emscripten/wasm mode of Bend's C runtime
(https://github.com/bendlang/bend/issues/1598).

